 "seesTitle": "What Cap de Formentor sees",
 "seesHTML": "From the cape the sun descends over open sea with \u22482.5\u00b0 of clearance, and the crescent reaches the water with the Formentor cliffs at your back. It is also the most logistically fraught green spot on the island: one narrow serpentine road shared with every other eclipse chaser, and in summer the road normally carries evening access restrictions \u2014 verify the shuttle/closure rules for eclipse day before committing.",
 "capHTML": "Horizon panorama from Cap de Formentor: terrain silhouettes with projected angular altitudes against the sun's descent. The crescent is last visible at \u224820:48.",
 "recoTitle": "Around Cap de Formentor",
 "recoHTML": "<li><b>The lighthouse terraces</b> \u2014 classic, cramped, first to fill.</li><li><b>Talaia d'Albercutx</b> (back down the road, 358 m) \u2014 wide-open west view and an escape from the end-of-road crush.</li><li><b>Mirador des Colomer is blocked</b> \u2014 its own peninsula cliffs stand WNW; don't default to it.</li>",
 "verdictText": "Full show \u2014 totality, then the crescent sets into the sea at \u224820:48"
